      PEEL AND STICK VS SYNTHETIC VS FELT: CHOOSING ROOF UNDERLAYMENT THAT SURVIVES TAMPA BAY RAIN
    </h1>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ARTICLE -->
<article class="py-16">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 blog-content">

    <p>The shingle brand is what your neighbors see. The <strong>underlayment</strong> is what actually keeps your ceiling dry the day a shingle goes missing in a Tampa Bay squall. Between May and September, Pinellas and Hillsborough get somewhere north of 30 inches of rain, often 4 or 5 inches in a single afternoon, and the underlayment is the only layer standing between that water and your plywood deck.</p>

    <p>There are three real choices for a residential re-roof in Tampa Bay: <strong>ASTM D226 Type II 30 lb felt</strong> (the code-minimum baseline), <strong>synthetic underlayment</strong> (the modern default second layer), and <strong>self-adhered SBS peel-and-stick</strong> (the premium base layer that unlocks insurance credits). Every one of them has a place, and getting the layered combination right is one of the highest-leverage decisions you will make on a re-roof.</p>

    <h2>1. WHY UNDERLAYMENT ACTUALLY MATTERS IN TAMPA BAY</h2>

    <p>Underlayment does four jobs, in order of how often each one saves your ceiling:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Backup waterproofing when a shingle fails.</strong> Any lifted, cracked, or missing shingle exposes the underlayment to rain within hours. If the underlayment is intact, the ceiling stays dry until the shingle gets replaced. If the underlayment is torn or degraded, the water is inside your attic on the next storm.</li>
      <li><strong>Weather protection between tear-off and dry-in.</strong> A Tampa Bay re-roof spans one to three days. The underlayment has to keep the interior dry through any afternoon storm that rolls through before the shingles go on. This is where synthetic dramatically outperforms felt.</li>
      <li><strong>Secondary water resistance in a hurricane.</strong> If a named storm strips shingles from your roof, a full-deck self-adhered polymer underlayment can keep the framing dry for weeks until repairs happen. This is the OIR-B1-1802 credit worth $150 to $600 a year. See our <a href="/blog/wind-mitigation-inspection-tampa-bay">wind mitigation inspection guide</a> for the full credit stack.</li>
      <li><strong>Deck vapor barrier and separation.</strong> Prevents direct contact between the plywood deck and the asphalt shingles, which extends deck life and reduces resin extraction from the deck into the shingle mat.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Cheap out on this layer and you are betting your ceiling on the shingles never failing. In Tampa Bay, over a 15 to 20 year roof life, that is a bet you will lose at least once.</p>

    <h2>2. OPTION 1: 30 LB ASPHALT FELT (ASTM D226 TYPE II)</h2>

    <p>The oldest option, and still the code minimum under Florida Building Code Section 1518. Made of a cellulose or fiberglass mat saturated with asphalt, sold in 4 square rolls, tar-black, heavy, and vulnerable to sun exposure. It has been the standard residential underlayment since the 1920s.</p>

    <p><strong>Real Tampa Bay pros:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Cheapest option, roughly $22 to $32 per square installed.</li>
      <li>Meets minimum FBC 1518 requirements for asphalt shingle roofs.</li>
      <li>Some historic-district permits and a few insurance carrier specs still require it.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Real Tampa Bay cons:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Absorbs moisture. A wet 30 lb felt roll delivered to your driveway is 30 to 50 percent heavier than a dry one, and wrinkles ("fish mouths") appear once the sun hits it.</li>
      <li>Tears easily. Tear strength averages 20 to 40 pounds. A single misstep during install rips it.</li>
      <li>Degrades fast in UV. If shingles are delayed even 2 to 3 weeks, the felt is often beyond salvage and has to be replaced.</li>
      <li>Slippery when wet. Genuine safety issue during a Tampa Bay morning install after overnight condensation.</li>
      <li>Does not qualify for the Factor 4 secondary water resistance credit. You pay full wind premium.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Verdict: felt is fine as a code-minimum spec on a rental or a flip where the seller does not benefit from the insurance credit. On any owner-occupied Tampa Bay home, the incremental cost of synthetic is worth every dollar.</p>

    <h2>3. OPTION 2: SYNTHETIC UNDERLAYMENT</h2>

    <p>The current default second layer on virtually every Tampa Bay re-roof we install. Made of woven or spun polypropylene or polyethylene, typically 8 to 12 mils thick, sold in 10 square rolls, gray or tan, very light. It has replaced felt as the professional standard over the last 15 years for good reason.</p>

    <p><strong>Products we install on Tampa Bay homes:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>GAF Deck-Armor</strong>: breathable synthetic, part of the GAF Golden Pledge system that unlocks their 50-year warranty.</li>
      <li><strong>Owens Corning ProArmor</strong>: also breathable, walkable in wet conditions, ties to the OC Preferred Contractor warranty programs.</li>
      <li><strong>Polyglass Polyseal SA</strong> (used as base layer, see next section).</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Real Tampa Bay pros:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Tear strength 5 to 10 times higher than felt. Foot traffic and pop-nail resistance dramatically better.</li>
      <li>Does not absorb moisture. Rolls stay flat and light on the deck.</li>
      <li>UV rated for 60 to 180 days of exposure. Buys real time between dry-in and shingle install.</li>
      <li>Walkable in the wet. Safer for crews and less risk of slip damage.</li>
      <li>Lighter to handle. Faster install, sometimes half the labor of felt.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Real Tampa Bay cons:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Installed cost about $60 to $110 higher per square than felt. On a 25-square roof that is roughly $1,500 to $2,750 more.</li>
      <li>By itself (single layer, not over peel-and-stick) it does not qualify for the Factor 4 SWR credit on the wind mitigation form.</li>
      <li>Some off-brand synthetics are actually worse than felt. Stick to name-brand products from GAF, OC, Polyglass, or IKO.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Verdict: on any re-roof where peel-and-stick is not being used as the base layer, name-brand synthetic is the correct spec. On a re-roof with peel-and-stick base layer, synthetic makes an excellent second layer for redundancy on non-eave areas.</p>

    <h2>4. OPTION 3: SELF-ADHERED SBS PEEL-AND-STICK</h2>

    <p>The premium base layer, and the one that unlocks the biggest insurance credit stack. Made of a modified bitumen (SBS or APP) coating on a fiberglass or polyester mat with a self-adhesive underside, sold in 1 to 2 square rolls, gray or granulated, very sticky. Historically used only at eaves and valleys as ice-and-water shield. In Florida, it is increasingly used across the entire deck.</p>

    <p><strong>Products we install on Tampa Bay homes:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Polyglass Elastoflex SA V (full-deck self-adhered)</strong>: SBS-modified, granulated, self-adhered, FL product approved. This is our default full-deck peel-and-stick on Tampa Bay homes chasing the SWR credit.</li>
      <li><strong>GAF WeatherWatch and StormGuard</strong>: eaves and valleys, or full deck depending on spec.</li>
      <li><strong>Owens Corning WeatherLock G Granulated</strong>: OC's granulated SBS peel-and-stick option, works with OC Preferred Contractor warranty programs.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Real Tampa Bay pros:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Self-seals around nail penetrations. This is the killer feature. Even if a shingle nail backs out, the SBS closes around the shank and keeps water out.</li>
      <li>Full-deck application unlocks the OIR-B1-1802 Factor 4 SWR credit. Roughly $150 to $600 a year in Tampa Bay insurance savings.</li>
      <li>Sticks directly to the deck. Wind uplift resistance is dramatically better than mechanically fastened underlayment.</li>
      <li>SBS-modified for flexibility across Tampa Bay's 80-degree daily thermal swings. Does not crack in winter cold snaps or bake brittle in July.</li>
      <li>Standard practice in HVHZ (Miami-Dade, Broward). If it is what the strictest wind zone in the country requires, it is what Tampa Bay should be spec'ing too.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Real Tampa Bay cons:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Installed cost $400 to $900 higher per roof than synthetic-only. On a 25-square roof that is roughly $10,000 to $22,500 base underlayment cost.</li>
      <li>Requires clean, dry deck. Cannot be installed in rain or over wet plywood.</li>
      <li>Requires mid-install photo documentation for the wind mit inspector. If your roofer forgets, the SWR credit is lost.</li>
      <li>Once down, it is permanent. Any future partial repair requires cutting through the SBS and patching with the same product.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Verdict: on any Tampa Bay home where the owner plans to file a wind mitigation inspection (which should be every Tampa Bay home), full-deck peel-and-stick pays back through insurance savings inside 12 to 30 months. On homes where the owner does not intend to file the wind mit form, the math is closer, but the leak resilience alone is usually worth the upcharge.</p>

    <h2>5. THE TWO-LAYER SYSTEM WE RECOMMEND FOR TAMPA BAY</h2>

    <p>Our default spec on any owner-occupied Tampa Bay re-roof is a <strong>two-layer underlayment system</strong>:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Base layer:</strong> Polyglass Elastoflex SA V or equivalent SBS peel-and-stick across 100 percent of the deck. Locks in SWR credit and self-seals every nail penetration.</li>
      <li><strong>Secondary layer:</strong> Name-brand synthetic (GAF Deck-Armor or OC ProArmor) across the field. Provides redundant weather protection during install, backs up any peel-and-stick installation errors, and ties to manufacturer warranty programs.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Total added cost over felt-only: $600 to $1,400 on a typical 25 to 30 square Tampa Bay home. Insurance savings over 5 years: $750 to $3,000. Leak-defense value: incalculable, but the day a shingle disappears in a July storm you will care very much.</p>

    <h2>6. SPECIAL CASES: TILE AND METAL ROOFS</h2>

    <p>Everything above assumes an asphalt shingle roof. For tile and metal, the underlayment story shifts:</p>

    <p><strong>Concrete or clay tile in Tampa Bay:</strong> the tiles themselves last 50 to 75 years, but the underlayment is your only water barrier and it typically fails at 20 to 25 years. Because you never want to re-underlay a tile roof twice, we spec heavy-duty double-layer peel-and-stick from day one. Products like Polyglass Polystick TU Plus and TU MAX are rated for tile applications specifically and last the full life of the tiles. Cost is significantly higher than shingle underlayment but the payback is not needing a mid-life underlayment replacement.</p>

    <p><strong>Standing seam metal in Tampa Bay:</strong> underlayment matters less than on shingle because the metal panels are much less likely to fail, but a high-temperature-rated peel-and-stick is still standard because the metal can hit 180 degrees on a July afternoon and standard peel-and-stick will melt or slide. Products like GAF FeltBuster HT or Polyglass Polystick MTS Plus are rated for the temperatures behind Gulf Coast Supply and TriCounty Metals panels. See our <a href="/blog/metal-roof-benefits-florida">metal roof benefits guide</a> for the full metal spec.</p>

    <h2>7. QUESTIONS TO ASK YOUR TAMPA BAY ROOFER ABOUT UNDERLAYMENT</h2>

    <p>Underlayment is one of the easiest places for a lowball estimate to hide a downgrade. Before you sign, ask the following:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>What specific underlayment products are in the quote?</strong> Brand name and product line, not "synthetic" or "peel-and-stick" generically.</li>
      <li><strong>Is peel-and-stick going full deck or only eaves and valleys?</strong> Full deck unlocks the SWR credit. Eaves-only does not.</li>
      <li><strong>Will you photograph the underlayment before the shingles go on?</strong> Required proof for the wind mit inspector. If they say no, the SWR credit is at risk.</li>
      <li><strong>Which manufacturer warranty program does the spec qualify for?</strong> GAF Golden Pledge, GAF System Plus, OC Platinum Protection, OC Preferred Protection. Different underlayment specs unlock different warranty tiers.</li>
      <li><strong>What is the FL Product Approval number?</strong> Every underlayment sold for use in Florida has a FL Product Approval number. It should be listed on the material submittal for your permit.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>A quote that itemizes brand, product, and application area is one you can trust. A quote that says "high-quality underlayment included" and nothing else is a quote you should ask harder questions about. See our <a href="/blog/gaf-vs-owens-corning">GAF vs Owens Corning comparison</a> for how the underlayment ties to the shingle warranty program.</p>

    <h2>8. THE UNDERLAYMENT WORTH ASKING FOR</h2>

    <p>If you take one thing away from this guide, make it this: on any Tampa Bay re-roof after 2026, the correct default spec is <strong>full-deck SBS peel-and-stick base layer plus name-brand synthetic second layer</strong>. That combination costs roughly $600 to $1,400 more than felt-only, saves $150 to $600 a year in insurance premiums, self-seals every nail penetration, buys you dry framing if a hurricane strips shingles, and unlocks the top-tier manufacturer warranty programs.</p>

    <p>Any Tampa Bay roofer who cannot quote that spec, or who tells you it is overkill, is either cost-cutting to hit a lowball number or is not paying attention to what the FBC and OIR-B1-1802 credit stack actually reward in 2026.</p>

    <p>Happy Roof installs both single-layer synthetic and full-deck peel-and-stick systems on every Tampa Bay re-roof under Florida license CCC1337380. Call <a href="tel:8135957663" class="call-tracking-number">(813) 595-7663</a> or <a href="/contact">book a free roof consultation</a> and we will walk you through the underlayment spec line by line before you commit.</p>

  </div>
</article>

